gpu_profile_2026-09-17_rtx2080.tsv

Where the GPU time of one synthesis goes, per kernel, before and after the tuning of 711233ad.
This is the file the tuning was chosen from, and the before / after pair quoted in
gpu/README.md: both sides were run back to back on an otherwise idle card, each size in a fresh
context, so they are comparable in a way the size sweep (which drives the card continuously and
therefore at sustained clocks) is not.

adapter: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2080 [Vulkan DiscreteGpu; NVIDIA 580.178.04]
wgpu: 30.0.1
host: dev (Ryzen 9 9950X3D), Ubuntu, kernel 7.0.0-31-generic
commands: gpu_bench --adapter "NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2080" --profile --rounds 5
  "before" from a binary whose gpu/ is 417be423, "after" from 0120c800. Each side is the
  *second* run of its binary: the first run after a build measures the card at low clocks and
  is not comparable (it inflated an earlier SOP "before" by 20%).
commit: 0120c800

Method: `Workspace::set_profile(true)` records each dispatch as its own compute pass with a
begin / end timestamp pair, so `device_ms` is the device's own clock per kernel, summed over the
dispatches of one picture and taken as the median of five pictures. Splitting the pass costs
almost nothing here — the summed total is within 1% of the same picture timed as a single pass
(`gpu_bench --profile` prints both) — but it is still a measurement mode, not how the decoder
runs. Rows are the per-kernel aggregate; the raw per-dispatch table is 136 KB and regenerable
with `just gpu-profile`.

columns: side (before / after), op, width, height, kernel (the pipeline key: kernel, geometry,
  output blocks per invocation, fused activation, fused ReLU6 on the input, fused second
  operand), dispatches in one picture, device_ms summed over them, pct of the picture,
  picture_ms for the whole picture.

What it showed, HOP 1024x1024 (635.3 ms before): 1x1 convolution 162.4 ms in 35 dispatches,
depthwise 3x3 134.4 ms in 20, the 3x3 family 231.6 ms in 27, transposed 3x3 stride 2 36.7 ms in
6, the ELU gate 25.7 ms in 8, all of attention 20.3 ms in 32. So the tuning went after the
depthwise (memory-bound: one multiply-add per 16 bytes read — 134.4 -> 64.8 ms), the transposed
convolution (three quarters of its taps were multiplied out by a divisibility test — 36.7 ->
28.1 ms) and the workgroup edge (1x1 convolutions 162.4 -> 117.3 ms), and the fused gate /
residual removed dispatches everywhere. Whole picture 635.3 -> 498.0 ms; SOP does not move at
all, for the reasons in gpu/README.md.

What it says is still wrong, and is the next job: the convolutions run at a few per cent of the
card's f32 peak, and the tuning has not changed that. HOP's biggest single dispatch, the 3x3
stride-2 of the CAB (256x256 output, 128 channels in and out, 9.66 G multiply-adds), takes
49.1 ms before and 44.6 ms after: 0.39 TFLOP/s rising to 0.43, against roughly 10 TFLOP/s of
fp32. Weight traffic does not explain it (the invocations of a workgroup read the same weight
blocks, i.e. a broadcast) and neither does DRAM bandwidth (about 0.4 bytes per multiply-add
reaches the card's memory, a quarter of what 450 GB/s would allow). Finding out what does —
occupancy, warp stall reasons — comes before writing another kernel. Note that the CUDA
profilers cannot do it: these are Vulkan compute shaders, `ncu` reports "No kernels were
profiled" on this binary and the `nsys` installed here cannot load its Vulkan importer. Nsight
Graphics (GPU Trace) is the right tool and is not installed on this box; until it is, the
per-dispatch timestamps in this file and A/B runs of kernel variants are the measurement.
